DU(1)                          P32W Unix                           DU(1)

NAME
       du - summarize disk usage

SYNOPSIS
       du [-as] [name ...]

DESCRIPTION
       du walks each name (by default the current directory) and
       everything below it, and prints for each directory the
       number of kilobytes used in it and under it, followed by its
       name. Subdirectories come before the directories that hold
       them, so the last line for a name is its total. A name that
       is a plain file prints its own size.

       Sizes are in 1024-byte blocks (the filesystem's block size;
       see fs(5)), counted per file: the file's size rounded up to
       whole blocks, plus the indirect blocks a file of that size
       needs (one beyond 8 blocks, more beyond 264). Devices count
       as 0. A file with several links is counted once, at the
       first of its names that du meets.

OPTIONS
       -a     print a line for every file too, not only for
              directories.
       -s     print only the total for each name.
       -k     accepted and ignored: sizes are always in KiB.
       --     ends the options.

EXAMPLES
       du /usr/ken                   ken's space, directory by
                                     directory
       du -s /usr/*                  a total for each home
       du / | sort -rn | head        the biggest directories

EXIT STATUS
       0 if everything was examined; 1 if something could not be
       (a name that does not exist, a directory that cannot be
       read: each is reported, and counted as its own size); 2 for
       an unknown option.

SEE ALSO
       df(1), ls(1), find(1), sort(1), fs(5)

BUGS
       A file with holes is counted as if it had none. Only the
       first 500 files with several links are remembered; others
       are counted at each name.
